ot/risingwave/-/milestones/19/edit)\\n  *     * Delete \\n\\n  * <::after>\\n\\n[ release-2.0 ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/20)\\n\\nAdministrator / risingwave \\n\\nexpired on Aug 15, 2024 \\n\\nExpired \\n\\n117/127 complete \\n\\n92% \\n\\nMilestone actions \\n\\n  * [ Close  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/20?milestone%5Bstate_event%5D=close)\\n  * [ Edit  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/20/edit)\\n  *     * Delete \\n\\n  * <::after>\\n\\n[ release-2.1 ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/21)\\n\\nAdministrator / risingwave \\n\\nexpired on Oct 15, 2024 \\n\\nExpired \\n\\n98/115 complete \\n\\n85% \\n\\nMilestone actions \\n\\n  * [ Close  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/21?milestone%5Bstate_event%5D=close)\\n  * [ Edit  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/21/edit)\\n  *     * Delete \\n\\n  * <::after>\\n\\n[ release-2.2 ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/22)\\n\\nAdministrator / risingwave \\n\\nexpired on Nov 30, 2024 \\n\\nExpired \\n\\n18/231 complete \\n\\n7% \\n\\nMilestone actions \\n\\n  * [ Close  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/22?milestone%5Bstate_event%5D=close)\\n  * [ Edit  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/22/edit)\\n  *     * Delete \\n\\n  * <::after>\\n\\n[ future-release-2.4 ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/24)\\n\\nAdministrator / risingwave \\n\\nOpen \\n\\n0/4 complete \\n\\n0% \\n\\nMilestone actions \\n\\n  * [ Close  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/24?milestone%5Bstate_event%5D=close)\\n  * [ Edit  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/24/edit)\\n  *     * Delete \\n\\n  * <::after>\\n\\n[ future-release-2.3 ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/23)\\n\\nAdministrator / risingwave \\n\\nOpen \\n\\n0/44 complete \\n\\n0% \\n\\nMilestone actions \\n\\n  * [ Close  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/23?milestone%5Bstate_event%5D=close)\\n  * [ Edit  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/23/edit)\\n  *     * Delete \\n\\n  * <::after>\\n\\n[ release-1.9 ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/18)\\n\\nAdministrator / risingwave \\n\\nOpen \\n\\n103/108 complete \\n\\n95% \\n\\nMilestone actions \\n\\n  * [ Close  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/18?milestone%5Bstate_event%5D=close)\\n  * [ Edit  ](/root/risingwave/-/milestones/18/edit)\\n  *     * Delete \\n\\n\\n\\n', url='http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/risingwave/-/milestones', trigger_by_action='browse', error=False, observation='browse', goal_image_urls=[], open_pages_urls=['http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/risingwave/-/milestones'], active_page_index=0, last_browser_action='goto(\"http://the-agent-company.com:8929/root/risingwave/-/milestones\")', last_browser_action_error='', focused_element_bid='70'), CmdRunAction(command='cd /workspace/risingwave && grep -RInE \"ingest|ingestion|backpressure|flush loop|flush|buffer saturation|buffer|ownership|owner|team|assignee|Li | Li$\" --exclude-dir=.git docs src | head -n 200', is_input=False, thought='', blocking=False, hidden=False, action='run', confirmation_state='confirmed', security_risk=None), CmdOutputObservation(content='docs/dev/src/metrics.md:36:Next, high barrier latency could also be caused by buffers in the graph.\\ndocs/dev/src/metrics.md:37:If some downstream buffer is congested, we will be unable to queue and continue processing upstream messages.\\ndocs/dev/src/metrics.md:41:This means the upstream executor will be unable to continue processing new stream messages, until some space on the buffer is freed.\\ndocs/dev/src/metrics.md:43:The various buffer sizes can currently be adjusted via options in the developer configuration file.\\ndocs/dev/src/metrics.md:44:For instance, options to configure buffer size of the exchange executor can be found [here](https://github.com/risingwavelabs/risingwave/blob/a36e01307d60491b91870ac5a37049a378fe986f/src/config/example.toml#L49-L50).\\ndocs/dev/src/metrics.md:46:Another subtle cause is that large buffer size can also worsen barrier latency.\\ndocs/dev/src/metrics.md:48:Typically, backpressure kicks in, the source is throttled.\\ndocs/dev/src/metrics.md:49:If buffer sizes are too large, or if there are many buffers, there will not be backpressure applied to source immediately.\\ndocs/dev/src/SUMMARY.md:44:    - [Shared Buffer](./design/shared-buffer.md)\\ndocs/dev/src/contributing.md:64:- If there is a conflict, it will open an issue and make the original author the assignee.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/checkpoint.md:12:Internally, the upcoming changes may take a while to propagate from sources to materialized views, and at least one barrier event is required to flush the changes. Such two kinds of latency determine the latency between write and read.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/checkpoint.md:32:   - For other operators, the barrier messages flow through the operators and trigger a checkpoint operation on them. Namely, flush the changes into storage.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/checkpoint.md:33:3. When all dirty states from a compute node are flushed to storage, the compute node sends a finish signal to the meta service.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/checkpoint.md:40:A local shared buffer is introduced to stage these uncommitted write batches. Once the checkpoint barriers have pass through all actors, the storage manager can notify all compute nodes to \\'commit\\' their buffered write batches into the shared storage.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/checkpoint.md:42:![shared buffer](../images/checkpoint/shared-buffer.svg)\\ndocs/dev/src/design/checkpoint.md:44:Another benefit of shared buffer is that the write batches in a compute node can be compacted into a single SSTable file before uploading, which significantly reduces the number of SSTable files in Layer 0.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/streaming-overview.md:59:This procedure guarantees that every state to be flushed into the storage is consistent (matching a certain barrier at the source). Therefore, when querying materialized views, consistency is naturally guaranteed when the batch engine reads a consistent snapshot (of views and tables) on the storage. We also call each barrier an epoch and sometimes use both terms interchangeably as data streams are cut into epochs. In other words, the write to the database is visible only after it has been committed to the storage via the checkpoint.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/streaming-overview.md:61:To improve the efficiency, all dirty states on the same compute node are gathered to a shared buffer, and the compute node asynchronously flushes the whole shared buffer into a single SST file in the storage, such that the checkpoint procedure shall not block stream processing.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:26:For the first option, it is not feasible to buffer all the updates, when historical data takes while to process.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:27:If we buffer all the updates it can cause OOM.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:94:So we didn\\'t need to buffer all the updates, until historical data is completely processed.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:182:we use a `Replicated StateTable` to read historical data, and replicate the shared buffer.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:266:If we poll a chunk from the upstream side, we will buffer it.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:272:we will need to flush the upstream chunk buffer.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:273:First, transform the schema of the upstream chunk buffer from 2. to 3..\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:274:Next we will flush records which are lower or equal to the pk we have backfilled to.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:284:from the shared buffer for the previous epoch.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:288:we can\\'t rely on the shared buffer of upstream,\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:293:So we need to make sure for the previous epoch, we buffer\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:294:its updates somewhere to replicate the shared buffer.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:296:Then we can merge the shared buffer, with the current\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:300:To replicate the shared buffer, we simply just create a `ReplicatedStateTable`.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:303:merging the shared buffer and the committed data in the Object store for us.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/backfill.md:344:merging the shared buffer with the committed data in the Object Store.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/state-store-overview.md:31:The Hummock itself provides 3 simple APIs: `ingest_batch`, `get`, and `scan`. Hummock provides MVCC write and read on KV pairs. Every key stored in Hummock has an *epoch* (aka. timestamp). Developers should specify an epoch when calling Hummock APIs.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/state-store-overview.md:33:Hummock doesn\\'t support writing a single key. To write data into Hummock, users should provide a ***sorted, unique*** list of ***keys*** and the corresponding ***operations*** (put value, delete), with an ***epoch***, and call the `ingest_batch` API. Therefore, within one epoch, users can only have one operation for a key. For example,\\ndocs/dev/src/design/state-store-overview.md:131:Every operation on the LSM-tree yields a new ***version*** on the Hummock manager, e.g., adding new L0 SSTs and compactions. In streaming, each stream barrier is associated with an ***epoch***. When the barrier flows across the system and collected by the stream manager, we can start doing ***checkpoint*** on this epoch. SSTs produced in a single checkpoint are associated with an ***uncommitted epoch***. After all compute nodes flush shared buffers to shared storage, the Hummock manager considers the epoch committed. Therefore, apart from the list of files in LSM, a version also contains committed epoch number `max_committed_epoch` and SSTs in uncommitted epochs. As a result, both an ***operation on LSM*** and a ***streaming checkpoint*** will yield a new ***version*** in the Hummock manager.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/state-store-overview.md:153:Here we have two cases: Agg executors always persist and produce new write batches when receiving a barrier; Join executors (in the future when async flush gets implemented) will produce write batches within an epoch.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/state-store-overview.md:157:Streaming executors cannot control when data will be persisted \u2014 they can only write to Hummock\\'s `shared buffer`. When a barrier flows across the system and is collected by the meta service, we can ensure that all executors have written their states of ***the previous epoch*** to the shared buffer, so we can initiate checkpoint process on all worker nodes, and upload SSTs to persistent remote storage.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/state-store-overview.md:159:For example, the barrier manager sends barrier epoch = 2. When the epoch 2 barrier is collected on meta service, we can ensure that data prior to epoch 2 have been fully flushed to the Hummock shared buffer. (Note that epoch number in streaming is generated by machine time + serial number, so ***we cannot simply use +1 -1 to determine the epoch of the previous / next barrier***.) Assuming the previous barrier is of epoch 1, we can start checkpointing data from epoch 1 after barrier of epoch 2 has been collected.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/mv-on-mv.md:39:6. The Chain operator consumes all messages from snapshot and receives EOF, then consumes buffered messages from upstream.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/relational-table.md:26:State Table provides the table operations by these APIs: `get_row`, `scan`, `insert_row`, `delete_row` and `update_row`, which are the read and write interfaces for streaming executors. The Mem Table is an in-memory buffer for caching table operations during one epoch. The Storage Table is read only, and will output the partial columns  upper level needs.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/relational-table.md:31:To write into KV state store, executors first perform operations on State Table, and these operations will be cached in Mem Table. Once a barrier flows through one executor, executor will flush the cached operations into state store. At this moment, State Table will covert these operations into kv pairs and write to state store with specific epoch.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/shared-buffer.md:7:> Note: L0 and Shared buffer are totally different. They both exist independently.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/shared-buffer.md:17:  - The shared buffer will generate SST based on epoch, and provide a consistent view of a epoch, by merging the snapshot of the storage SSTs and the immutable in-memory buffers.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/shared-buffer.md:19:- Support read-after-write (so-called async flush), so as to make executor logic simpler.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/shared-buffer.md:23:  2. Write to in-memory flush buffer, and merge data from flush buffer and storage (like TopN, HashJoin).\\ndocs/dev/src/design/shared-buffer.md:28:it will flush its content to Hummock using the write_batch interface.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/shared-buffer.md:35:and flush them as one SST to the storage engine.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/shared-buffer.md:95:- merge-on-read: Write to in-memory flush buffer, and merge data from flush buffer and storage (like TopN, HashJoin).\\ndocs/dev/src/design/shared-buffer.md:100:it will always need to \u201cmerge data from flush buffer and storage\u201d.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/shared-buffer.md:104:Therefore, to standardize this, we support \u201casync flush\u201d in shared buffer,\\ndocs/dev/src/design/architecture-design.md:86:The durability of materialized views in RisingWave is built upon a snapshot-based mechanism. Every time a snapshot is triggered, the internal states of each operator will be flushed to S3. Upon failover, the operator recovers from the latest S3 checkpoint.\\ndocs/dev/src/design/data-model-and-encoding.md:33:In-memory data is encoded in arrays for vectorized execution. For variable-length data like strings, generally we use another offset array to mark the start of encoded values in a byte buffer.\\nsrc/java_binding/src/hummock_iterator.rs:103:            prefetch_buffer_capacity: 1 << 10,\\nsrc/java_binding/make-java-binding.toml:48:[tasks.ingest-data-and-run-java-binding]\\nsrc/java_binding/make-java